<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_drop_constraint.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-dropconstraint">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-dropconstraint">
  <primary>DROP CONSTRAINT</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>DROP CONSTRAINT</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>DROP CONSTRAINT</refname>
  <refpurpose>remove a constraint from a label</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
DROP CONSTRAINT <replaceable class="parameter">constraint_name</replaceable> ON <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable>
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropconstraint-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>DROP CONSTRAINT</command> removes one constraint from one label. It takes both
   forms that <xref linkend="cypher-createconstraint"/> creates: the exclusion constraint
   behind <literal>IS UNIQUE</literal>, and the check constraint behind a condition. It also
   drops the index that a unique constraint owns.
  </para>

  <para>
   The statement is a small one. The synopsis above is the whole of it.
  </para>

  <para>
   The name is required. There is no way to name a property and drop whatever constraint is
   on it, so <literal>DROP CONSTRAINT ON film</literal> is a syntax error at
   <literal>ON</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   There is no <literal>IF EXISTS</literal>.
   <literal>DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS ...</literal> is a syntax error, so a constraint that
   is not there is always an error.
  </para>

  <para>
   There is no <literal>CASCADE</literal> and no <literal>RESTRICT</literal>, and no name
   list. One statement drops one constraint.
  </para>

  <para>
   Neither name can be qualified. The label is looked up in the graph named by
   <varname>graph_path</varname>.
  </para>

  <para>
   The statement reaches any named constraint on the label's table, not only the ones Cypher
   created. The label's primary key is one of them. Read the name before running it.
  </para>

  <para>
   A property index is not a constraint. Drop one with
   <xref linkend="cypher-droppropertyindex"/>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropconstraint-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">constraint_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name of the constraint to drop, unqualified. The psql commands
      <literal>\dGv</literal> and <literal>\dGe</literal> report it, and so does the
      <literal>pg_constraint</literal> catalog.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Label the constraint is on, in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropconstraint-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>. They
   build a graph of their own and drop it at the end. Four constraints are created, two of
   them unnamed:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH rulebooks;
SET graph_path = rulebooks;
CREATE VLABEL film;
CREATE CONSTRAINT film_title_uniq ON film ASSERT title IS UNIQUE;
CREATE CONSTRAINT ON film ASSERT year IS UNIQUE;
CREATE VLABEL teen;
CREATE CONSTRAINT teen_age ON teen ASSERT (10 &lt;= age::int AND age::int &lt;= 19);
CREATE CONSTRAINT ON teen ASSERT age &gt; 9;
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Drop a unique constraint. The constraint and its index both go, and the unnamed one
   created after it stays:
<programlisting>
DROP CONSTRAINT film_title_uniq ON film;

DROP CONSTRAINT
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Running it a second time is an error, because there is no
   <literal>IF EXISTS</literal> to soften it:
<programlisting>
DROP CONSTRAINT film_title_uniq ON film;

ERROR:  constraint "film_title_uniq" of relation "film" does not exist
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   And the softer spelling does not parse:
<programlisting>
DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS film_title_uniq ON film;

ERROR:  syntax error at or near "EXISTS"
LINE 1: DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS film_title_uniq ON film;
                           ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A constraint created without a name has a derived one.
   <literal>pg_constraint</literal> lists every constraint on the label's table:
<programlisting>
SELECT conname FROM pg_constraint WHERE conrelid = 'rulebooks.teen'::regclass ORDER BY conname;

         conname
--------------------------
 teen_age
 teen_id_not_null
 teen_pkey
 teen_properties_check
 teen_properties_not_null
(5 rows)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>teen_age</literal> is the named check constraint and
   <literal>teen_properties_check</literal> the derived one. The other three belong to the
   label's own shape. Both check constraints are dropped by name:
<programlisting>
DROP CONSTRAINT teen_properties_check ON teen;
DROP CONSTRAINT teen_age ON teen;

DROP CONSTRAINT
DROP CONSTRAINT
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A property index is not a constraint, whatever it is named:
<programlisting>
CREATE PROPERTY INDEX film_year_idx ON film (year);
DROP CONSTRAINT film_year_idx ON film;

ERROR:  constraint "film_year_idx" of relation "film" does not exist
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   And the name cannot carry the graph:
<programlisting>
DROP CONSTRAINT rulebooks.film_unique_constraint ON film;

ERROR:  syntax error at or near "."
LINE 1: DROP CONSTRAINT rulebooks.film_unique_constraint ON film;
                                 ^
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>DROP GRAPH rulebooks CASCADE</literal> drops the graph the examples built.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropconstraint-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   <command>DROP CONSTRAINT</command> takes any named constraint on the label's table, and a
   label's primary key is one of them.
   <literal>DROP CONSTRAINT film_pkey ON film</literal> succeeds and leaves the label without
   its primary key on <literal>id</literal>, and no statement puts that back. Check the name
   against <literal>\dGv</literal> or <literal>pg_constraint</literal> first, and keep to the
   names <xref linkend="cypher-createconstraint"/> produced.
  </para>

  <para>
   To change a constraint, drop it and create it again.
   <literal>CREATE OR REPLACE CONSTRAINT</literal> is refused.
  </para>

  <para>
   A check constraint that a child label inherited can only be dropped from the label that
   declares it. Given
   <literal>CREATE VLABEL feature INHERITS (work)</literal>, naming the child is refused with
   <literal>cannot drop inherited constraint "work_y" of relation "feature"</literal>.
   Dropping the constraint from <literal>work</literal> removes it from the whole family.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropconstraint-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no constraint DDL, so nothing there corresponds to this statement.
   Its way of constraining a graph is a graph type, which is dropped whole by the statement
   in clause 12.7.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>DROP CONSTRAINT ... ON <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></literal>
   corresponds to
   <literal>ALTER TABLE ... DROP CONSTRAINT ...</literal>, with the label name after the
   constraint name instead of before it. It offers less: no <literal>IF EXISTS</literal>, no
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> or <literal>RESTRICT</literal>, and no way to drop several
   constraints in one statement. It reaches exactly as far, though, since any named
   constraint on the label's table is the same set <command>ALTER TABLE</command> would
   reach.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

